<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  studio-build-loop DRIVER LaunchAgent. Runs drive.sh ONCE at load (RunAtLoad),
  KeepAlive=false so a clean stop-condition exit stays stopped (no thrash-restart).
  drive.sh internally loops run.sh (fresh headless claude per piece) until a signal
  issue / stall / cap. This is the headless continuous driver -- no interactive
  session in the loop.
  Load:   launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.autonomy.studio-build-driver.plist
  Stop:   launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.autonomy.studio-build-driver.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.autonomy.studio-build-driver</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/lukebradford/Dev/studio-loop/drive.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/lukebradford/Dev/studio-loop-repo</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>

    <!-- DELIBERATELY NO STUDIO_QUOTA_URL. THIS COPY pinned it to 8080 (added by
         3a17fe1, the C2 PR). The pin never took effect, because this file is a
         versioned copy and the INSTALLED agent predates it and carries no pin --
         but it was a trap rather than a dead letter: an EnvironmentVariables
         entry BEATS drive.sh's `${VAR:-default}`, so the first time anyone
         copied this file over the installed one, every later change to that
         default would have gone silently inert. The port now lives in exactly
         one place, drive.sh's default (8788, the supervised service from #765
         Defect 2), and `test_install_studio_server.sh` asserts the installer
         agrees with it. Do not re-add a pin here. -->
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <false/>
  <!-- RETRY CADENCE: every hour, via StartInterval rather than a handful of
       wall-clock ticks.

       OPERATOR MODEL (2026-08-05, in their words): "The loop should just run so
       long as it's not above 80% of the weekly, it should back off if a 5 hour
       window is hit and start again when cleared. After the weekly 80% quota is
       hit, it should be able to pick up once that is cleared."

       Two of those three already hold inside drive.sh and need nothing from
       launchd: a 5-hour/rate limit is caught per-fire and BACKED OFF IN-PROCESS
       ("retries automatically when the limit clears"), and MAX_FIRES=0 means no
       per-window fire cap. The gap was only ever the THIRD: the weekly gate makes
       the driver EXIT, and nothing then retried until the next wall-clock tick.
       Measured 2026-08-05: the window reset at 02:59, 55 minutes after the 02:05
       tick had read 96% and stopped, so the next attempt was 21:05 -- 18h idle on
       a loop that was behaving perfectly.

       An hourly StartInterval is the direct expression of "just keep trying":
       resumption is bounded by an hour after ANY stop cause clears -- weekly
       quota, an operator signal being closed, or a drained queue refilling --
       instead of by where the next fixed tick happens to fall. It replaces the
       03:05/21:05 pair (and a 4-tick variant that was proposed but never asked
       for) rather than adding to them; two mechanisms for one job is how the
       03:05-vs-21:05 drift in #765 happened.

       COST OF A WASTED TICK IS ~10s AND ZERO TOKENS: the quota gate runs BEFORE
       the auth probe and before any fire, so a tick that finds the window still
       exhausted logs one refusal and exits. There is no work-hours exposure to
       trade off, because a tick only ever STARTS a STOPPED driver -- and a
       started driver already runs continuously for days (the 2026-07-31 run
       spanned 30h). QUOTA_STOP_PCT=80% is what protects the operator's own
       headroom, not the hour a tick lands on.

       A tick on an already-running driver is a launchd no-op (single instance per
       label) -- verified, not assumed: that 30h run spanned three scheduled ticks
       and launchd started no second driver.

       KeepAlive stays FALSE. StartInterval retries on a fixed cadence we control;
       KeepAlive would restart the instant the process exits, which turns a clean
       stop-condition exit into a thrash loop.

       To disable: launchctl unload this. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>3600</integer>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/lukebradford/Dev/studio-loop/logs/driver.launchd.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/lukebradford/Dev/studio-loop/logs/driver.launchd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
